\section*{Completeness}

Write $A=36n^{3}-19-12mn$, so the equation reads $A^{2}-y^{2}=8m^{3}$, i.e.\
$d'e'=2m^{3}$ with $d'=\frac{A-y}{2}$, $e'=\frac{A+y}{2}$. Every solution with
given $m\neq0$ arises from a signed divisor pair of $2m^{3}$, with $n$ an
integer root of $36n^{3}-12mn-(A+19)=0$ (forcing $A\equiv5\pmod{12}$).
Enumerating all divisor pairs for all $m$ in a range (segmented-sieve
factorization, tandem pair generation, residue-tracked filters, exact
$128$-bit root tests) is exhaustive in that range with $n$ unbounded.

\begin{theorem}
The table lists every nontrivial integer solution of
$y^{2}=(36n^{3}-19-12mn)^{2}-(2m)^{3}$ with $1\le|m|\le10^{9}$. Exactly two
rows lie outside this window --- rows $31$ and $39$ --- and both are torsion
translates (see below) of in-window rows $32$ and $40$ respectively.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Stratified completeness to $10^{10}$]
The only solutions with $1\le|m|\le10^{10}$ whose divisor pair contains an
element of absolute value $\le 64$ are rows $20$ and $32$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Positive-$m$ classification]
If $m>0$ then $8m^{3}\le(36|n|^{3}+12m|n|+19)^{2}$, whence $m\le24n^{2}$ for
$|n|\ge2$ (and $m\le25$ for $|n|=1$). Since $24\cdot6454^{2}<10^{9}$, for
every $|n|\le6454$ the positive-$m$ solutions in the table are complete:
they are rows $4,8,11,15,25,27,29,30,34,36$ and no others, and fibers
absent from this list (e.g.\ $n=1$) have no positive-$m$ solutions at all.
\end{corollary}

\section*{The torsion-translate structure}

For fixed $n$, with $X=-2m$, $Y=y$, $c=36n^{3}-19$, the equation is
$E_{n}\colon Y^{2}=X^{3}+36n^{2}X^{2}+12ncX+c^{2}$, and $T=(0,c)$ ---
corresponding to the trivial family $m=0$ --- is a section of exact order
$3$. Translation by $T$ yields the explicit map (for the appropriate sign
$\varepsilon\in\{\pm1\}$ of $y$)
\begin{equation*}
m'=-\frac{1}{2}\left[\left(\frac{\varepsilon y-c}{2m}\right)^{2}
-36n^{2}+2m\right],
\end{equation*}
which carries solutions to solutions whenever integrality and parity
survive. This pairs up rows
$19\leftrightarrow20$, $14\leftrightarrow15$, $31\leftrightarrow32$,
$33\leftrightarrow34$, and $39\leftrightarrow40$. In particular the giant
row $31$, $|m|\approx5.4\times10^{12}$, is the $T$-translate of the modest
row $32$, and row $39$ ($|m|\approx2.6\times10^{9}$) is the $T$-translate of
the sweep-discovered row $40$. The pairs on $n=19$ and $n=-4$ are
\emph{not} translates: those points are independent in the free part,
consistent with $\operatorname{rk}E_{19}=3$ and $\operatorname{rk}E_{-4}=2$.

